      "content": "Proponents of biological hierarchy often cite studies on IQ scores, such as those by Arthur Jensen, who argued that IQ is heritable and that certain racial groups have average IQ scores that are significantly different from others. They may also reference the work of Richard Lynn and Tatu Vanhanen, who published a book claiming that IQ scores are strongly correlated with racial group membership. In terms of neuroanatomical differences, they might point to studies on cranial capacity, such as those by Robert J. Berry, who claimed that there are significant differences in cranial capacity between different racial groups. They may also cite studies on brain structure, such as those on the size and shape of the corpus callosum, which they claim are correlated with intelligence. Additionally, they might reference the work of J.P. Rushton, who argued that there are genetic differences in the structure of the brain that are associated with intelligence and behavioral traits.",
