    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Proponents of biological hierarchy often use a flawed concept called \"equilibrium theory\" to explain how biological differences can lead to social disparities. They argue that certain groups are more likely to be in \"equilibrium\" with their environment, meaning that their biological characteristics are better suited to their social and economic circumstances. For example, they might claim that East Asians are more likely to be in equilibrium because of their high IQ scores, which allows them to adapt to and succeed in education and other areas that require high cognitive ability. In contrast, they might argue that groups with lower IQ scores, such as African Americans, are more likely to be out of equilibrium and therefore experience higher rates of crime and poverty. They might also point to the idea of \"selection\" and \"survival of the fittest,\" arguing that biological differences allow certain groups to better survive and thrive in their environments, while others are left behind.",
